# Design Decision: SDK Choice for Published Agent Application Endpoint

## Date: 2026-03-18

## Status: Accepted

## Context

The `website/chatui` app currently uses the `Azure.AI.Projects` SDK (v1.2.0-beta.5) to interact
with a Foundry Agent via a **project-scoped endpoint**. The app relies on:

- **Server-managed conversations** via `CreateProjectConversationAsync()`
- **Conversation-scoped Responses client** via `GetProjectResponsesClientForAgent(agent, conversationId)`

We are migrating to a **published Agent Application endpoint** — a stable, production-ready Azure
resource with its own identity, RBAC, and invocation URL. This endpoint only exposes the
**stateless Responses API** (`POST /responses`). The following APIs are not available:
`/conversations`, `/files`, `/vector_stores`, `/containers`.

This is a temporary limitation (Microsoft plans to add end-user data isolation), but it is the
current production reality as documented at:
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/agents/how-to/publish-responses

The key constraint: the published endpoint speaks the **OpenAI Responses API protocol**.

## Design Principle

**Purity = protocol-only.** The client SDK should match the abstraction level of the endpoint
it talks to. The published endpoint strips away the platform layer (project management, agent
lifecycle, conversation management) — those concerns are handled by the Agent Application
resource itself via Azure Resource Manager. What remains for the caller is a raw
OpenAI-compatible `POST /responses` endpoint.

## SDK Architecture Understanding

There is a layered SDK architecture in the .NET ecosystem for Azure AI:

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  Microsoft Agent Framework  (orchestration)      │
│  AIAgent, RunAsync(), memory, tools, multi-turn  │
│                                                  │
│  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  Microsoft.Extensions.AI  (abstraction)    │   │
│  │  IChatClient                               │   │
│  │                                            │   │
│  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────┐   │   │
│  │  │  OpenAI .NET SDK  (protocol)         │   │   │
│  │  │  ResponsesClient, ChatClient         │   │   │
│  │  └─────────────────────────────────────┘   │   │
│  └───────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                  │
│  Azure.AI.Projects  (platform)                   │
│  AIProjectClient, ProjectConversation,           │
│  Agent management (wraps OpenAI SDK internally)  │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

- **OpenAI .NET SDK** — protocol layer. Speaks the OpenAI wire format. GA, co-maintained by
  OpenAI and Microsoft (repo: openai/openai-dotnet). `Azure.AI.Projects` depends on it
  transitively.
- **Azure.AI.Projects** — platform layer. Adds project scoping, agent management,
  conversation management on top of the OpenAI SDK.
- **Microsoft.Extensions.AI** — abstraction layer. Vendor-neutral `IChatClient` interface.
  Currently modeled on Chat Completions, not the Responses API.
- **Microsoft Agent Framework** — orchestration layer. Provider-agnostic agent runtime
  (`AIAgent`, `RunAsync()`, multi-agent, tools, memory). Uses Extensions.AI and OpenAI SDK
  underneath.

During **development** (project endpoint), the platform layer is needed: create agents, manage
conversations, connect to data sources. In **production** (application endpoint), the platform
concerns are handled by Azure Resource Manager. The caller only needs the protocol layer.

## Candidates Evaluated

### A: Azure.AI.Projects (current SDK) — ❌ No-Go

We are migrating away from the project endpoint. Every API we currently use (`AIProjectClient`,
`ProjectConversation`, `GetProjectResponsesClientForAgent`) is project-scoped and does not work
on the application endpoint. Staying on this SDK saves zero migration effort — the controller
must be rewritten regardless — and leaves us carrying a beta dependency designed for an endpoint
we are no longer targeting.

### B: OpenAI .NET SDK — ✅ Decision

The published endpoint is explicitly OpenAI-compatible. Microsoft own docs show calling it with
the `openai` library + custom base_url. The `OpenAI` NuGet package is:

- **GA** (not beta/prerelease)
- **Co-maintained** by OpenAI and Microsoft (announced at Build 2024, stable since Oct 2024)
- **Native Responses API support** via `OpenAI.Responses` namespace and `OpenAIResponseClient`
- Already imported transitively by our current `Azure.AI.Projects` dependency
- Supports custom base URL + credential, which is exactly how application endpoints work

If Microsoft later lifts the stateless restriction and adds conversations back to application
endpoints, those will arrive via the Responses API — the same protocol this SDK speaks.

### C: Azure.AI.Inference — ❌ No-Go

Built around the Chat Completions protocol (`ChatCompletionsClient`). The published endpoint
speaks the Responses protocol — different wire format entirely. Wrong tool for this endpoint.

### D: Microsoft.Extensions.AI (IChatClient) — ❌ No-Go

`IChatClient` is modeled on Chat Completions (`CompleteAsync` with `ChatMessage`). There is no
`IResponseClient` or Responses API abstraction in Microsoft.Extensions.AI yet. Would need a
custom adapter that defeats the purpose of using a standard abstraction.

### E: Plain HttpClient — ⚠️ Possible but wasteful

Works — it is just HTTP. But we would reimplement auth token lifecycle, request serialization,
response deserialization, and retry logic that the OpenAI SDK already provides. No advantage
over Option B.

### F: Microsoft Agent Framework — ⚠️ Reassess (test required)

The Agent Framework is a provider-agnostic runtime that produces a standard `AIAgent` with
`RunAsync()`, streaming, sessions, and tool support. It has **multiple providers**, and our
original assessment focused on the wrong one.

#### Original assessment (March 18): Foundry provider — ❌ No-Go

We initially evaluated the **Foundry Agents provider** (`Microsoft.Agents.AI.AzureAI`), which
uses `PersistentAgentsClient` / `AIProjectClient`. These clients hardcode project URL path
construction, resulting in 404s when given an application endpoint URL.

Issue microsoft/agent-framework#2722 tracked this gap. The discussion evolved:

- **Dec 9** (@rogerbarreto, MS Agent Framework team): provided a glass-breaking workaround
  using URL surgery, custom pipeline policy for `api-version`, and JSON patching to remove
  the `model` field.
- **Dec 11** (@trrwilson, Azure SDK team): clarified that `ProjectResponsesClient` already
  accepts application endpoint URIs directly — no glass-breaking needed. Factory method
  tracked at Azure/azure-sdk-for-net#54426.

This path works but routes through `Azure.AI.Projects` (beta) and still requires the `model`
field patch.

#### Reassessment (March 25): Azure OpenAI provider — the right framing

The published agent application endpoint exposes **standard OpenAI Responses API**. This is
not a Foundry-specific protocol — it is the same wire format as any Azure OpenAI deployment.
The Agent Framework has a dedicated **Azure OpenAI provider** (`Microsoft.Agents.AI.OpenAI`)
with a Responses client that speaks this protocol natively:

    // docs: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/agent-framework/agents/providers/azure-openai
    // NuGet: Azure.AI.OpenAI, Azure.Identity, Microsoft.Agents.AI.OpenAI (all prerelease)

    AzureOpenAIClient client = new AzureOpenAIClient(
        new Uri(applicationEndpoint),      // the published agent URL
        new DefaultAzureCredential());

    var responsesClient = client.GetResponseClient(modelDeploymentName);

    AIAgent agent = responsesClient.AsAIAgent(
        instructions: "...",
        name: "baseline-chatbot");

    Console.WriteLine(await agent.RunAsync("Hello"));

This is the **same `AzureOpenAIClient`** we already use in Option B. The Agent Framework
just wraps it with `.AsAIAgent()` to provide:

- `RunAsync(prompt)` — string in, string out (no manual `ResponseItem[]` construction)
- `RunStreamingAsync()` — built-in streaming
- `create_session()` — built-in multi-turn session management
- Function tools, tool approval, code interpreter, file search, web search, hosted MCP

The key realization: we do NOT need the Foundry provider at all. The published endpoint IS an
Azure OpenAI endpoint. The Azure OpenAI provider is the correct entry point.

#### Comparison: Option B vs Option F (Azure OpenAI provider)

| Aspect                 | Option B (current)                             | Option F (Azure OpenAI provider)              |
|------------------------|------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|
| Base client            | `AzureOpenAIClient`                            | Same `AzureOpenAIClient`                      |
| Response client        | `client.GetResponseClient(model)`              | Same, then `.AsAIAgent()`                     |
| Calling DX             | `CreateResponseAsync(ResponseItem[])` — manual | `RunAsync("prompt")` — string in, string out  |
| Multi-turn             | Manual history accumulation in JS              | Built-in session management                   |
| Streaming              | Manual `CreateResponseStreamingAsync`           | `RunStreamingAsync()` built-in                |
| Tool support           | Manual tool handling                           | Function tools, MCP, code interpreter, search |
| Custom base URL        | First-class                                    | Same — inherits from `AzureOpenAIClient`      |
| Extra NuGet            | None beyond `Azure.AI.OpenAI` + `OpenAI` (GA) | `Microsoft.Agents.AI.OpenAI` (prerelease)     |
| Model field            | Must pass model name                           | Must pass model name — **needs testing**      |

#### Open questions (must test before deciding)

1. **Does `AzureOpenAIClient` accept the full application endpoint URL?** Our URL is:
   `https://{account}.services.ai.azure.com/api/projects/{project}/applications/{app}/protocols/openai`
   The Azure OpenAI provider docs show the standard resource endpoint format
   (`https://<myresource>.openai.azure.com`). The application URL has a different structure.
   Does `AzureOpenAIClient` route correctly, or does it append paths that break?

2. **Model field handling.** The application endpoint bakes the model in. `GetResponseClient()`
   requires a model/deployment name. What happens if we pass a dummy value? Does the endpoint
   ignore it, or does it 400? If it fails, the `Patch.Remove("$.model")` workaround from
   issue #2722 would be needed — but that was demonstrated on the Foundry provider path, not
   the Azure OpenAI provider path.

3. **Auth audience.** Our current Option B implementation sets the token audience to
   `https://ai.azure.com`. Does `AzureOpenAIClient` use this audience by default for
   Foundry-hosted endpoints, or does it default to the Azure OpenAI audience
   (`https://cognitiveservices.azure.com`)? If the latter, we need to configure it.

4. **api-version.** The application endpoint requires `api-version=2025-11-15-preview` in the
   query string. Does `AzureOpenAIClient` inject this, or does it use a different default?

#### What this means for our decision

If all four questions resolve positively — the URL routes correctly, the model field is
handled, auth works, and api-version is correct — then Option F via the Azure OpenAI provider
becomes a compelling upgrade over Option B:

- Same base client, same auth, same URL handling
- Dramatically better DX: `RunAsync("prompt")` vs building `ResponseItem[]` arrays
- Built-in session management replaces our manual JS history accumulation
- Streaming, tools, MCP support come for free
- The only cost is one additional prerelease NuGet package

If any question fails, Option B remains the correct choice — it is GA, tested, and working
in production today.

#### Historical context: Foundry provider path (issue #2722)

For reference, the Foundry provider path (`Microsoft.Agents.AI.AzureAI`) also works but
routes through `Azure.AI.Projects` (beta) and was initially presented as requiring
glass-breaking workarounds. The Azure SDK team later clarified that `ProjectResponsesClient`
accepts application URIs directly. A factory method is planned (Azure/azure-sdk-for-net#54426).
This path is viable but unnecessarily complex for our use case — the Azure OpenAI provider
is the simpler, more direct route.

## Decision

**Use the OpenAI .NET SDK (Option B)** as the primary SDK for consuming the published Agent
Application endpoint.

### Rationale

1. **Protocol purity** — the SDK operates at the same abstraction level as the endpoint
   (protocol-to-protocol, no unused platform abstractions)
2. **GA maturity** — not beta, co-maintained by OpenAI and Microsoft
3. **Microsoft alignment** — this is what Microsoft own docs recommend for published endpoints
4. **Future-proof** — any features added to the application endpoint will arrive via the
   Responses API, which this SDK natively supports
5. **Zero wasted abstractions** — no dead code from project-scoped APIs we do not need
6. **Stability over DX** — the Agent Framework offers a nicer calling surface
   (`RunAsync(prompt)`) and CAN reach the application endpoint via a workaround, but the
   workaround relies on URL surgery, custom pipeline policies, and JSON patching against
   undocumented SDK internals. The OpenAI SDK supports custom base URLs as a first-class,
   documented feature. We chose stability over marginal DX improvement.

### Migration Impact

| Component | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| NuGet packages | Azure.AI.Projects (beta) + Azure.Identity | OpenAI (GA) + Azure.Identity |
| DI registration | `AIProjectClient` singleton | `OpenAIResponseClient` singleton |
| Config | `AIProjectEndpoint` + `AIAgentId` | `ApplicationEndpoint` (agent baked in) |
| Conversations | Server-managed (`ProjectConversation`) | Client-managed (send full history) |
| Controller | `GetProjectResponsesClientForAgent()` | Direct `CreateResponseAsync()` |
| Frontend JS | Stores threadId, sends single message | Accumulates messages, sends full array |

### Consequences

- Client must manage conversation history for multi-turn conversations
- When Microsoft lifts the stateless restriction, we can add server-side conversation support
  back via the same SDK
- If client-side orchestration needs arise, Microsoft Agent Framework can be layered on top

### Status Update (March 25, 2026)

Option B is implemented, tested, and deployed. PR #91 is open and ready for review.

However, reassessment of Option F revealed the **Azure OpenAI provider**
(`Microsoft.Agents.AI.OpenAI`) as a potentially cleaner path that was not evaluated in the
original decision. This provider wraps the same `AzureOpenAIClient` we already use, adding
`RunAsync()` DX, built-in sessions, and tool support. See Option F section above for details.

The decision stands pending testing. If the Azure OpenAI provider works against our published
endpoint (URL routing, model field, auth audience, api-version all resolve), a follow-up PR
migrating from raw `ResponsesClient` to `responsesClient.AsAIAgent()` would be a small,
low-risk change — same base client, better DX.

